## 🐧 Linux Basics

```bash
pwd                      # Print Working Directory: where am I?
ls -la                   # List all files (-a includes hidden ones) with details (-l)
cd /path/to/folder       # Change directory (cd .. goes up one level)
cat file.txt             # Print the contents of a file
less file.txt            # Scroll through a long file (q to quit)
head -n 20 file.txt      # Show the first 20 lines
tail -n 20 file.txt      # Show the last 20 lines
file mystery_file        # Identify what type of file it really is
man ls                   # Open the manual page for a command
history                  # Show previous commands you ran
```

### Searching

```bash
grep "keyword" file.txt          # Find lines containing "keyword"
grep -rni "keyword" .            # -r search folders recursively, -n show line numbers, -i ignore case
find . -name "*.txt"             # Find files by name in the current folder
find / -name "notes.txt" 2>/dev/null   # Search the whole system; 2>/dev/null hides "permission denied" noise
```

### Permissions

```bash
ls -l file.sh            # Shows permissions like -rwxr-xr--  (owner / group / others)
chmod +x file.sh         # Make a file executable
chmod 644 file.txt       # Owner: read+write, everyone else: read only
```

Permission numbers: `r = 4`, `w = 2`, `x = 1`. So `7 = rwx`, `6 = rw-`, `4 = r--`.

### Working with text

```bash
wc -l file.txt           # Count lines
sort file.txt | uniq     # Sort lines, then remove duplicates (| "pipes" output into the next command)
cut -d',' -f2 data.csv   # Split by comma and print column 2
tr 'a-z' 'A-Z'           # Translate characters (here: lowercase to uppercase)
```

### Remote access (only to servers provided by the CTF)

```bash
ssh username@host -p 2222        # Connect over SSH; -p sets the port
scp username@host:file.txt .     # Copy a file from the remote server to the current folder
```

