## 🔎 Forensics

```bash
file evidence.bin                 # Identify the real file type from its contents
exiftool photo.jpg                # Read metadata (author, dates, camera, GPS, etc.)
strings evidence.bin | less       # Print readable text inside a binary file
strings -n 8 evidence.bin         # Only show strings at least 8 characters long
xxd evidence.bin | head           # Hex dump: view the first bytes ("magic bytes" show file type)
binwalk evidence.bin              # Scan for embedded files inside a file
binwalk -e evidence.bin           # -e extracts anything it finds
foremost -i evidence.bin -o out/  # Carve files out of a disk image or blob
sha256sum evidence.bin            # Record a hash so you can prove the file wasn't changed
```

**Network captures:**

```bash
tshark -r capture.pcap                          # Print packets from a capture file
tshark -r capture.pcap -Y "http"                # -Y applies a display filter (here: HTTP only)
tshark -r capture.pcap -Y "http" -T fields -e http.host -e http.request.uri   # Show specific fields
```

Or open the file in **Wireshark** and use filters such as `http`, `dns`, `ip.addr == 10.0.0.5`, or *Follow → TCP Stream*.

> Best practice: work on a **copy** of the evidence and record its hash first.

